#doc (Manual) "Downsets as quotients" =>

This section proves that $`F_0` is not dense.  It computes the downsets of
$`t_1, \ldots, t_5` exactly, as finite tables, and reads seven covers off them.
The method is that a downset of $`F_0` is a quotient of $`F_0`, so it is
generated by its bottom element and a finite set closed under the generating
operations is all of it.  The results are in `wip/f0Covers.lean`.

# The reduction lemmas

For a Heyting algebra $`H` with a nucleus $`j` and $`a \in H`, the relation
$`x \equiv y \iff x \land a = y \land a` is a congruence, and $`x \mapsto x \land a`
maps $`H` onto the downset $`{\downarrow}a = \{x : x \le a\}`.  The induced
operations on $`{\downarrow}a` are $`\land`, $`\lor` and $`\bot` as they are, with
$`a` as top, $`l \Rightarrow_a l' = (l \twoheadrightarrow l') \land a` as
implication and $`j_a(l) = \bigcirc l \land a` as nucleus.  In PLL this is three
interderivabilities, one for each connective that does not commute with
$`\land a` on the nose.  PROVED.

