# The finite-downset theorem

Since $`F_0` is generated by $`\bot`, so is each quotient $`{\downarrow}a`.  In
Lean the closure conditions are a structure: a list $`L` of formulas contains
$`\bot` and $`a`, lies below $`a`, and is closed up to $`\dashv\vdash` under
$`\land`, $`\lor`, $`l \mapsto \bigcirc l \land a` and
$`(l, l') \mapsto (l \twoheadrightarrow l') \land a`.

{stmt}`DownTable`

{docstring DownTable +allowMissing}

{srcLink}`DownTable`

Under such a table every variable-free $`x` has $`x \land a` interderivable with
a member of $`L`, by induction on $`x` with one reduction lemma per connective;
so every variable-free formula below $`a` is interderivable with a member.
PROVED.

