The first two say that on $`{\downarrow}t_5` the induced nucleus $`j_{t_5}` sends
$`t_2` to $`t_3` and fixes $`t_4`; the third is the corresponding fact for
$`t_4`.

That the members of each table are pairwise distinct is checked on one
constraint model with four worlds: the ladder worlds $`0, 1, 2` and a fallible
world above them, with a modal step from $`0` to the fallible world.  On
$`\{0, 1, 2\}` the truth sets of $`t_0, \ldots, t_5` are
$`\emptyset, \{0\}, \{1\}, \{0, 1\}, \{0, 2\}, \{0, 1, 2\}`, which are pairwise
distinct, and soundness turns each separation into an underivability
(`D1_distinct`, …, `D5_distinct`).  As a poset $`{\downarrow}t_5` is the bottom of
the Rieger–Nishimura ladder:

```
          t5
         /  \
       t3    t4
      /  \  /
    t2    t1
      \  /
       t0
```

# The covers

A cover $`u \lessdot v` in $`F_0` is $`u < v` with nothing strictly between.

{stmt}`Covers`

{docstring Covers +allowMissing}

{srcLink}`Covers`

A table for $`{\downarrow}v` with $`u` a member gives $`u \lessdot v` once every
other member is shown not to lie above $`u`: every $`x` between $`u` and $`v` is
interderivable with a member of the table, and the underivabilities checked
on the four-world model rule out all members but $`u` and $`v`.  The seven edges
of the diagram above are covers of $`F_0`, so $`F_0` is not dense.  PROVED.

